\par\par \b \uc2  ASIA MARKETS-Taiwan tension becomes war of words. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Richard Hubbard \par \uc2 986 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 1999\par 11:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, July 19 (Reuters) - Asian stocks and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  markets had mixed fortunes on Monday with most interest centered \par \par \uc2  on the tension between Taiwan and China. \par \par \uc2      Taiwan's stock and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets actually ended firmer on \par \par \uc2  the day, but due largely to some hefty government verbal and \par \par \uc2  actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some bargain hunting also helped the share \par \par \uc2  market. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CHINA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -TAIWAN DECLARE WAR OF WORDS \par \par \uc2      China's president Jiang Zemin made his first comments on the \par \par \uc2  rising tension between the two sides on Sunday. \par \par \uc2      Speaking on Sunday to U.S. President Bill Clinton, the \par \par \uc2  Chinese leader warned that Beijing has not ruled out the use of \par \par \uc2  force against Taiwan, even though its basic policy is to reunify \par \par \uc2  peacefully with Taipei. \par \par \uc2      President Jiang was also quoted by the Xinhua news service \par \par \uc2  as saying "there are certain forces in Taiwan and the \par \par \uc2  world...that are attempting to separate Taiwan" from \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2      And he said \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not tolerate any foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  in the issue. \par \par \uc2      For his part, Clinton was quoted as repeating that US \par \par \uc2  policy, which recognizes Beijing as the representative of \par \par \uc2  One-China, has not changed. \par \par \uc2      "At this point China is trying to appear as restrained as \par \par \uc2  possible, and it's going to engage in a war of words for \par \par \uc2  sometime," said Andrew Xie, North Asia Economist at Morgan \par \par \uc2  Stanley Dean Witter in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2      Neal Stovicek, head of research at National Securities in \par \par \uc2  Taiepi, said the war of words could last for sometime. \par \par \uc2      "Over the medium term through October, we see a low \par \par \uc2  intensity conflict, primarily verbal, with the U.S. pushing for \par \par \uc2  peaceful cooperation," Stovicek said. \par \par \uc2      TAIWAN SET TO HOLD 'STATE-TO-STATE' POLICY \par \par \uc2      Taiwan President Lee Teng-hui's new policy of calling for \par \par \uc2  state-to-state negotiations between the two sides has found \par \par \uc2  support with voters. \par \par \uc2      A survey published over the weekend in Taiwan newspapers \par \par \uc2  showed 43 percent support for the two-state policy. This \par \par \uc2  compares to just 28.3 percent who said they oppose the stated \par \par \uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  Most Taiwan survey respondents also said Beijing, not Lee, \par \par \uc2  is to blame for the strained relations between the two sides \par \par \uc2  since last week. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN AUTHORITIES PLEDGE STABILITY \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has pledged to maintain the stability \par \par \uc2  of the New Taiwan dollar and interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's deputy governor said Taiwan's economic \par \par \uc2  fundamentals are sound and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to \par \par \uc2  monitor any "abnormal" transactions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Separately, Taiwan's finance ministry said Saturday it has \par \par \uc2  authorised big state-backed funds to boost their equity holdings \par \par \uc2  by about 360 billion Taiwan dollars. \par \par \uc2  The three major public pension funds and the Taiwanese \par \par \uc2  postal fund are now authorized to invest up to 50% of their \par \par \uc2  assets into equities. \par \par \uc2      Taiwan's weighted index, the TAIEX, ended the day barely \par \par \uc2  changed, up just 0.28 percent. \par \par \uc2      SOUTH KOREA'S DAEWOO COULD FACE BANKRUPTCY \par \par \uc2      South Korea's financial watchdog said the country's second \par \par \uc2  largest chaebol, the Daewoo Group, may go bankrupt if it can't \par \par \uc2  roll over a hefty mountain of short-term debt coming due. \par \par \uc2      Daewoo said it has about $6 billion of short term debt \par \par \uc2  paper to mature in the next 10 days. And the conglomerate is \par \par \uc2  offering creditors up to $8.5 billion of collateral to back \par \par \uc2  fresh longer term bonds to support the rollover. \par \par \uc2      The collateral includes around $1 billion of securities \par \par \uc2  donated by Daewoo Group chairman Kim Woo-choong. \par \par \uc2      Daewoo's troubles and the spectre of rising interest rates \par \par \uc2  has unsettled Korea's markets with Treasury bond yields rising \par \par \uc2  and the stock market falling slightly. \par \par \uc2      REGIONAL STOCKS END MIXED \par \par \uc2      The tension between China and Taiwan contributed to a \par \par \uc2  weaker session on the Hong Kong stock market, where the key \par \par \uc2  Hang Seng index finished down 0.72 percent. \par \par \uc2      Philippine shares ended down around one percent as \par \par \uc2  investors deserted the market, awaiting a clearer picture on \par \par \uc2  interest rate trends. \par \par \uc2      Across southeast Asia markets were correcting after recent \par \par \uc2  strong rallies, and share indexes ended lower across the board. \par \par \uc2      Change on the day MON 19 0923GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...Latest bid....Prev Close....Pct Move \par \par \uc2   Ringgit......3.7995.......3.8000..........0.01% \par \par \uc2   Rupiah......6700.00......6715.00..........0.22% \par \par \uc2   Baht..........37.19........37.11.........-0.20% \par \par \uc2   Peso..........38.32........38.25.........-0.18% \par \par \uc2   Sing dlr.....1.7038.......1.7012.........-0.15% \par \par \uc2   Taiwan dlr...32.330.......32.370..........0.12% \par \par \uc2   Korean won..1187.50......1182.30.........-0.44% \par \par \uc2   Japan yen....120.27.......120.81..........0.45% \par \par \uc2   Change since start of Asia crisis \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .....Latest......July '97......Pct Move \par \par \uc2   Ringgit......3.7995.......2.5200........-33.68% \par \par \uc2   Rupiah......6700.00......2432.00........-63.70% \par \par \uc2   Baht..........37.18........25.90........-30.34% \par \par \uc2   Peso..........38.32........26.50........-30.85% \par \par \uc2   Sing dlr.....1.7038.......1.4300........-16.07% \par \par \uc2   Taiwan dlr..32.3300......27.7500........-14.17% \par \par \uc2   Korean won..1187.50.......842.00........-29.09% \par \par \uc2   Japan yen....120.27.......114.40.........-4.88% \par \par \uc2  ************************************************ \par \par \uc2   STOCK MARKETS \par \par \uc2   Change on the day MON 19 0923GMT \par \par \uc2   Market........Current.....Prev Close.....Pct Move \par \par \uc2   Indonesia.....658.27.......662.37..........-0.6% \par \par \uc2   Malaysia......831.77.......843.12..........-1.3% \par \par \uc2   Philippines..2559.49......2585.69..........-1.0% \par \par \uc2   Singapore....2121.34......2134.22..........-0.6% \par \par \uc2   Thailand......481.49.......488.51..........-1.4% \par \par \uc2   Hong Kong...13447.13.....13545.24..........-0.7% \par \par \uc2   Japan.......18532.58.....18248.30...........1.6% \par \par \uc2   Taiwan.......7386.89......7366.23..