\par\par \b \uc2  EMERGING MARKETS-No wider panic from China spat. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Gill Tudor \par \uc2 620 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 1999\par 17:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 19 (Reuters) - A war of words between China and Taiwan has rattled some Asian markets but overseas analysts see little risk of wider emerging market impact. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan and Chinese stocks have plunged since Taipei said a week ago it was scrapping the "one China" policy that has helped maintain regional stability for decades, drawing a fierce response from Beijing. Shock waves have rippled across much of Asia, but elsewhere markets remain largely untouched. \par \par \uc2  "It's the silly season and people are just talking this thing up because they want to have a bit of volatility, like we saw with Argentina last week," said one emerging market strategist at a European bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Many emerging stock, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and debt markets took a pasting last week on a flurry of rumours that Argentina might devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or even default on its debts. \par \par \uc2  That sell-off proved short-lived as players decided there was no immediate Argentina crisis after all. Then Taiwan-China worries picked up steam, at least in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Markets are nervous that China might use military might against what it regards as independence moves in a renegade province. \par \par \uc2  Beijing has kept up a steady stream of threats and said on Sunday it did not rule out using force. During the last such crisis, in the run-up to Taiwan's first direct presidential election in March 1996, China fired missiles into Taiwan waters. \par \par \uc2  China-watchers say sabre-rattling on both sides looks set to persist for some time - perhaps even until Taiwan's next presidential election in March 2000 - but war is unlikely. \par \par \uc2  They say China has little stomach for the huge cost, in every sense, of an all-out conflict, while big business interests in Taipei will lean on their government to back off from a row that threatens their mainland investments. \par \par \uc2  The ping-pong game of Chinese threats and Taiwan defiance will gradually lose its market sting, and eventually blow over. \par \par \uc2  "It'll probably peter out over the next month or two, but it will be quite nervous for people in the meantime," said Nick Douch, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital in London. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's stock market plummeted 14 percent last week and only hefty government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state funds tugged it back from a further dip on Monday, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the Taiwan dollar. \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Shanghai's hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  B share index slumped 9.5 percent on Monday and the Shenzhen B index lost 8.8 percent. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong stocks were also edgy, losing 0.7 percent on the day, and traders said the spat cast a general shadow over regional equity and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said much of the selling had more to do with profit-taking than panic, following Asia's steep equity gains this year. \par \par \uc2  "Despite overriding positives there's a profit-taking mentality in Asia, and there's also a rising spectre that interest rates have bottomed," said Graham Neilson, Asia economist at Banque Paribas in London. \par \par \uc2  With prices knocked down, analysts and fund managers say some investors are ready to step back in and hunt for bargains. \par \par \uc2  Neilson said the region's recovery was increasingly underpinned by sound economics, with productivity on the increase, signs of rising volumes in the electronics industry - one of Taiwan's mainstays - and good company earnings. \par \par \uc2  "If you look at emerging markets as a whole, Asia has had a fantastic run based mainly on liquidity, but it still has a very strong fundamental position," he said. \par \par \uc2  ((London capital markets +44 171 542 6414, fax 583 7239, gill.tudor@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7j0438j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
