\par\par \b \uc2  =Taiwan Govt  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Calms Mkts, But Dangers Remain \b0\par\par\uc2  By Baker Li \par \uc2 657 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 1999\par 14:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TAIPEI (Dow Jones)--Taiwan government's aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  succeeded in calming badly shaken financial markets Thursday, but economists said they expect continued market volatility as fears over worsening cross-strait tensions persist. \par \par \uc2  "The tensions between the two sides of the Taiwan Strait won't subside so easily," said Ray B. Dawn, director of the Taiwan Institute of Economic Research. \par \par \uc2  Dawn referred to the furor resulting from Taiwan President Lee Teng-hui's remark that China and Taiwan should engage in "state-to-state" relations, instead of as separated parts of a single China. The comment has led to some stern warnings from China. \par \par \uc2  And Beijing's warnings are just the beginning, Dawn said. \par \par \uc2  Despite those dangers, the Taiwan government's stepped up calls for calm and the its active \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  returned the markets to near normal for at least one day. \par \par \uc2  Increasing government support-buying boosted Taiwan shares 0.37% Thursday, reversing three days of losses that clipped 7.7% from the main index. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the U.S. dollar ended nearly flat at NT$32.270 from NT$32.272 late Wednesday in quiet trading. Speculators were driven to the sidelines after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold US$300 million to US$400 million Wednesday to steady the New Taiwan dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was very effective," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a U.S. Bank. \par \par \uc2  Investors were encouraged by government moves to reassure markets that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue. Vice Finance Minister J.C. Wang said government funds will remain active in the market, and buy stocks at the appropriate time. The ministry also said those funds could be increased to NT$500 billion from NT$300 billion by September if the Legislature approves. \par \par \uc2  However, the threat of greater tensions remains. \par \par \uc2  "China's moves will be the market focus," said David Wu, vice president at President Securities Corp. "If things exacerbate, investors confidence will be damped further." It's possible the market may test 7,800 points in coming sessions, he said. \par \par \uc2  "Our government must show its determination, and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets effectively, or we aren't optimistic about the future direction of the markets," said Wu Chung-shu, a research fellow at the Institute of Economics of Academic Sinica, a government think tank. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par               Threat To Market Remains\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Chinese government leaders may lash out at Taiwan by canceling the\par planned visit by top Chinese envoy Wang Daohan to Taiwan for later this\par year, or China's military may threaten to use force against the island\par if things deteriorate, Dawn warned.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We're in a brief period of stability in the middle of a time of great\par instability," said Wu. "Today, the stock market recovered some of the\par ground it had lost over the past few days, but it's too early to\par celebrate. China must be thinking of some measures to deal with Taiwan's\par declaration of 'state-to-state' policy. More bad news may come out, and\par the local markets will be adversely affected."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, others said China would be reluctant to use military force on\par Taiwan because of the potential harm to China's own economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par China's economy suffered in 1995-1996 when China threatened Taiwan\par with missiles, which turned the average Taiwanese not against Taiwan's\par Lee, as Beijing had hoped, but against China, said Daniel Chen, the\par chief economist at the Industrial Bank of Taiwan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In 1995, China conducted the first of two missile tests in the waters\par near Taiwan to quash what Beijing viewed as attempts by Lee to advocate\par independence from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may intensify in light of comments\par from Shea Jia-dong, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  deputy governor, that the bank may\par "participate in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market adjustments," dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Baker Li; (8862) 2502-2557; bli@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  15-07-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1218GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1135 4201 55127 \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020010824dv7f00it1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
