\par\par \b \uc2  =Exclusive:Bk Of China: NT Dlr Seen Appreciating Further \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kim Frick \par \uc2 378 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 1999\par 01:25\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BASEL (Dow Jones)--Taiwan's large trade surplus and an increase in foreign investment will continue to push the New Taiwan dollar higher in the near term, the director general of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  department for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China told Dow Jones Newswires late Saturday. \par \par \uc2  "Fundamentally Taiwan's economy is good so the NT dollar should appreciate," said George A-Ting Chou, on the sidelines of the annual meeting of the Bank for International Settlements. \par \par \uc2  At 0430 GMT Saturday the U.S. dollar ended at NT32.742 up from NT32.737 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been supported by a $2.6 billion trade surplus in the first quarter of 1999 and a 2.6% increase in exports in the first four months of 1999, Chou said. \par \par \uc2  But he cautioned that Japan's sluggish economy could limit the rise of the NT dollar despite the fact the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has strong fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the Japanese yen will weaken further," Chou said. "Most of Asia's economies are on the track of recovery." \par \par \uc2  The NT dollar often moves in tandem with the yen, which was also weaker Saturday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar has been trading in a narrow range between NT$32.7 and NT$32.8 recently, as the yen has been quite steady and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain a stable Taiwanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Chou said Taiwan's economy is expected to grow by 4.3% in the first quarter of 1999 from 6.8% in the first quarter of 1998 while second quarter economic growth is seen at around 5%. \par \par \uc2  Chou said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China will continue its expansionary monetary policy in light of increased domestic demand and strong consumer consumption. \par \par \uc2  "I think our \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to take a more expansionary monetary policy to stimulate our economy," Chou said. \par \par \uc2  However, inflationary pressure in the U.S. has the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerned, particularly the U.S. Federal Reserve's shift to a tightening bias, Chou said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We are watching that," he said.\par    -By Kim Frick; 41 79 649 6990; kfrick@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  06-05-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1925EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010826dv6500zxf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
