\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan ends up, ignores BOC move. \b0\par\par\uc2 388 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 1999\par 07:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, June 3 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed slightly higher against the dollar on Thursday on technical buying that ignored concerns over a Bank of China halt to yuan settlements overseas, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan finished at its intraday high of 8.2783 to one dollar from 8.2786 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of China, a state-run commercial bank, announced it would stop yuan settlements at its branches outside China from June 10. The move unnerved bankers outside the country but had little impact on the tightly controlled domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of China said it would not allow inward remittances of yuan at its branches outside the country as of June 10. \par \par \uc2  The move, which would not affect inward remittances of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was aimed at plugging loopholes in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  management, the bank said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no evidence that the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Confusion over the move had raised new concerns on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets outside the country that China might be planning to devalue. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it would not devalue the yuan and bank officials repeated that pledge on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that they had received numerous telephone calls asking about the implications of the move but that there was no visible impact on trading. \par \par \uc2  "The yuan was pushed higher by technical buying today," said a dealer at a foreign bank. "There was no impact from the business change at the Bank of China." \par \par \uc2  The local unit was likely to move narrowly around current levels in the near term, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan touched an intraday low of 8.2787 on Thursday with most business done at 8.2786, they said. \par \par \uc2  Trade picked up slightly, they said. Turnover has slumped recently as many domestic banks offset customer demand internally without using the national market. \par \par \uc2  The weighted average price on Wednesday was 8.2787. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed higher against the Japanese yen at 6.7870 to 100 yen from Wednesday's 6.8898. It closed unchanged against the Hong Kong dollar at 1.0680 to one dollar. \par \par \uc2     ((Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 \par \par \uc2  reutersh@uninet.com.cn)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv63074wz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
