\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan down in morning, unaffected by NATO. \b0\par\par\uc2 217 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 1999\par 05:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, May 10 (Reuters) - China's yuan was down slightly against the dollar in late morning trade on Monday, but dealers there was little impact from NATO's mistaken bombing of the Chinese embassy in Belgrade. \par \par \uc2  By 0249 GMT, the yuan had edged down to 8.2788 against the dollar from Friday's close of 8.2780 on technical selling after solid gains last week, they said. \par \par \uc2  "Trade was thin and there was no panic in the market despite the bombing incident," said a dealer at a domestic bank. \par \par \uc2  Chinese leaders have condemned the United States over the incident while students have taken to the streets in protest. \par \par \uc2  "Trade was little affected as \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers do not expect a crisis despite the tension triggered by the bombing," said a dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  But China's reaction has raised some worries that both exports to other countries and foreign investment in China could slow, which could put pressure on the yuan, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no sign of the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the tightly-controlled market in early trade. \par \par \uc2  ((Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 reutersh@uninet.com.cn)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv5a01em5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
