\par\par \b \uc2  Chinese Yuan Ends Weaker As Importers' Dlr-Buying Continues \b0\par\par\uc2 357 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 05:20\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan closed weaker against the U.S. dollar Wednesday in tightly range-bound trading, as heavy dollar demand from importers continued, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S dollar closed Wednesday at CNY8.2798, up from CNY8.2795 after trading between CNY8.2795 and CNY8.2798. Traders said trade was valued around $100 million. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling dollars to keep the yuan steady below the CNY8.2800 level. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan is convertible under the current, or trade, account only. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is supported by a large trade surplus, which reached $43 billion last year, and hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of $146 billion. \par \par \uc2  The PBoC \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly in the market to maintain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a steady range, and to relieve concerns that the yuan might slip. The central government has pledged repeatedly not to devalue, despite more than a year of market rumors to the contrary. \par \par \uc2  "The (U.S.) \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is facing a bit of upward pressure because of seasonal demand from importers, who need dollars to buy equipment," said a local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with a state bank. "But fear of devaluation is unnecessary. Whatever happens to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is up to the government." \par \par \uc2  In other trading Wednesday, the Hong Kong dollar closed at CNY1.0677 from CNY1.0678, while the Japanese yen closed at CNY7.0750 per Y100, up from CNY6.9750 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, non-deliverable forwards fell Wednesday, signaling firmer belief in the Chinese government's pledge not to devalue the yuan from overseas traders, who use the NDFs as a hedging tool. \par \par \uc2  In the NDF market, money broker Exco Group in Singapore was quoting the U.S. dollar at CNY8.4892 for delivery in six months and at CNY8.9792 over a one-year timeframe. The six-month rate Wednesday carried a premium of CNY0.21 from CNY0.23 Tuesday and the one-year rate CNY0.70 from CNY0.78. \par \par \uc2  -By Joshua Cherin; (8621) 6218-3268; jcherin@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 24-03-99 \par \par \uc2  0420GMT \par \par \uc2  1153 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv3o02a0a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
