\par\par \b \uc2  China Yuan Ends Unchanged As Trade Continues To Contract \b0\par\par\uc2 312 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 05:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan closed unchanged in trading against the U.S. dollar Wednesday, as market dealings continued to contract, giving the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  even greater control of the local unit. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the yuan was stable, with capital inflows supporting the local unit. They also noted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was actively watching the market and may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on both the buy and sell side of the market to keep the unit steady. \par \par \uc2  But the traders also noted that total trading volumes appeared to have slipped again. Although trade volumes are only published once a month, participants say a change in the hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at state banks is crimping trade and giving the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  even greater ability to contllar closed Wednesday unchanged at CNY8.2787 after trading between CNY8.2786 and CNY8.2838. The yuan can be exchanged only for trade-related purposes. \par \par \uc2  Last year, China's largest state banks began revoking the hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading rights at local branch offices. Bank branches around China are now required to move all buy and sell orders through their headquarters in Beijing, where they are scrutinized for authenticity and then offset internally. The remaining orders, usually only a fraction of the original amount, are then shifted down to the Shanghai exchange, where then are transacted. \par \par \uc2  The measures were aimed at saving unnecessary trading costs at state banks and at shoring up loopholes through which dollars have illicitly entered and exited China. They were implemented just as capital flight was heating up, raising the prospect that China's large trade surplus wouldn't be enough to protect the yuan from devaluation pressures. China exported a net $43 billion more than the country imported last year. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  17-03-99 \par \par \uc2  0458GMT \par \par \uc2  1153 \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020010824dv3h00a3y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
