\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan ends up on Zhu's no devaluation pledge. \b0\par\par\uc2 404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 1999\par 06:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Jan 26 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed higher against the dollar in active trade on Tuesday, buoyed by a renewed pledge from premier Zhu Rongji not to devalue the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2780 to one dollar against 8.2783 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Zhu made his remarks about holding the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  steady during a meeting in Beijing with Laotian Prime Minister Keobounphan Sisavat. \par \par \uc2  "The Chinese government will continue to adhere to its policy of no devaluing the renminbi (yuan)," state television quoted Zhu as saying on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Zhu's remarks helped ease concerns over possible devaluation, said a dealer at local bank. \par \par \uc2  Other dealers said the yuan held fairly firm on the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market but renewed concerns had emerged since Brazil was forced to devalue. \par \par \uc2  The official Business Weekly on Sunday suggested that a devaluation or float of the yuan might not be a bad move, adding to market worries. \par \par \uc2  But on Monday an official at China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dismissed the report as a private opinion that did not reflect the views of the People's Bank of China. \par \par \uc2  "Zhu's remarks and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  statement reassured the market that there was no change in policy," said a dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  Beijing has repeatedly vowed not to devalue the yuan even though its exports showed sluggish growth. \par \par \uc2  Last August, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Shanghai-based \b \highlight22\uc2 China Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System to support the yuan, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a key member of the trading system and routinely guides the exchange rate but dealers said there were no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  the boost the yuan on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expected the yuan to continue to hold around present levels in the near term, aided by Beijing's no devaluation pledge and increased demand from local companies ahead of the Chinese lunar new year. The lunar new year begins on February 16. \par \par \uc2  The yuan hit an intraday high of 8.2779 and a low of 8.2791 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the weighted average price was 8.2784. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed down against the Japanese yen at 7.2800 to 100 yen against 7.2252 on Monday. It ended higher against the Hong Kong dollar at 1.0675 to one dollar from 1.0687. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1q05t0s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
