\par\par \b \uc2  China To List Euro On Shanghai Exch In 1H99-Official \b0\par\par\uc2  By Karby Leggett \par \uc2 160 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Dezember 1998\par 12:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China will list the new European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro, on its main foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange in Shanghai sometime during the first half of 1999, an exchange official said Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The move marks a vote of confidence for the euro, which will come into existence in January, analysts said. It will bring to four the number of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded on the Shanghai-based China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trading Center: the U.S. dollar, the Japanese yen, the Hong Kong dollar and the new euro. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan is traded only under the current, or trade account only. It's not convertible under the capital account, or for long-term investments. Dealings are also tightly controlled by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  daily to prevent sharply fluctuations in the local unit. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 23-12-98 \par \par \uc2  1157GMT \par \par \uc2  (CORRECTED 1219GMT) \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010917ducn02a8l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
