\par\par \b \uc2  Chinese Yuan Ends Up On Inflows;  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Caps Gains \b0\par\par\uc2 284 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 1998\par 06:17\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan (CNY) strengthened slightly against the U.S. dollar Monday, buoyed by robust trade and capital-investment inflows. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the large inflows brought an upswing in dollar selling and forced the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the market in order to prevent the yuan from surging. They added new upward pressure on the yuan is also the result of a sharp crackdown on illicit \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  outflows that has sealed up China's formerly leaky \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended Monday at CNY8.2777, down from CNY8.2781 in the previous session. It traded in a range of CNY8.2753 to CNY8.2779. Dealings were less than $200 million, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The yuan moved upward on technical factors, but there hasn't been a change to the market's fundamentals," a local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader said. He added that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market Monday in order to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from soaring. \par \par \uc2  The yuan is only convertible under the current, or trade, account. In addition, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -trading regulations require Chinese firms to repatriate all but 15% of their hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings. That, given China's huge trade surplus, boosts dollar selling. \par \par \uc2  Market participants said the yuan's upward trend will continue as long as the strict new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions are enforced, and as long as China continues to see strong net capital inflows. China's trade surplus hit $38.4 billion in the first 10 months of 1998. \par \par \uc2  Economists say they expect a net $60 billion surplus to flow into China this year from both trade and direct capital investment. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  16-11-98 \par \par \uc2  0517GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020010914dubg00ypb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
