\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan edges higher, buoyed by unified market. \b0\par\par\uc2 388 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Oktober 1998\par 05:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Oct 29 (Reuters) - The Chinese yuan closed up marginally against the dollar on Thursday, buoyed by a move to unify the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  After market close on Wednesday, state media reported that Beijing would abolish regional \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap centres on December 1, making them local branches of the Shanghai-based China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System, the national interbank market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the move would speed the flow of dolars onto the interbank system. \par \par \uc2  The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  finished the day at 8.2776 to one dollar from 8.2777 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said bringing swap centres them into the unified national system would speed up transactions by eliminating an intermediary agency and give the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more data on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions. \par \par \uc2  The centres were once a key source of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade for foreign companies though their role has diminished in recent years. Some dealers said they saw a limited impact from the move as foreign firms had largely used the settlement system of state banks since policy changes in 1996. \par \par \uc2  "The move convinced some domestic banks to buy the yuan today but we believe the impact will be limited," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan's rise on Thursday also reflected an official crackdown on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  abuses by domestic firms had led to an oversupply of dollars on the market, exerting upward pressure for the yuan. \par \par \uc2  There were no signs that the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market on Thursday but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen as likely to check the yuan's upward trend if the trend continued, they said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan has recently moved narrrowly around the record closing high of 8.2774 set on October 12. \par \par \uc2  The yuan hit an intraday high of 8.2772 and a high of 8.2778. Trade was dull with most business done at 8.2777, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The weighted average price on Wednesday was 8.2778. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed down against the Japanese yen at 7.0400 to 100 yen from 6.9812. It ended unchanged against the Hong Kong dollar at 1.0671 to one dollar. \par \par \uc2  - Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 \par \par \uc2  - Email: reutersh@uninet.com.cn. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926duat05tgj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
