\par\par \b \uc2  Hong Kong Stocks Gain 1% On Stronger Local Dollar, Rate-Cut Hopes \b0\par\par\uc2 566 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 1998\par 11:10\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- Hong Kong shares closed almost 1% higher Tuesday, lifted by a stronger Hong Kong dollar, expectations of interest-rate cuts overseas and massive buying of China-linked stocks. \par \par \uc2  The blue-chip Hang Seng index rose 71.61 points, or 0.9%, to close at 7733.47 after trading in a narrow range of 7672.64 and 7837.96. Monday, it rose 83.38 points, or 1.1%. \par \par \uc2  Trading volume rose to HK$5.55 billion, up from Monday's HK$3.7 billion. The broader All Ordinaries index finished up 40.44 points to 3460.43. \par \par \uc2  After next week's G-7 meeting of industrialized nations, investors are expecting rate cuts around the world, which is helping shares locally, said Lennon Chan, executive director of Tai Fook Securities Ltd. Investors expect the U.S. to take the lead, with other nations following, Chan said. \par \par \uc2  China-linked shares dominated trade Tuesday amid rumors of an impending rate cut. "About 40% of trading volume came from China plays," Chan said. \par \par \uc2  Apart from persistent rumors of a rate cut in China, investors were pleased by the latest economic data released in that country and news that floods, which have ravaged the nation this summer, appear to be over, Chan said. "As there was no government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  plays, all the negative news has been factored into their share prices," added Chan. \par \par \uc2  Chan was referring to the two weeks of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets that ended at the end of August. The government focused its buying on blue chip shares. There are only a couple of China-related shares in the Hang Seng index. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment also was boosted by a strong day on Wall Street, where the Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 149.85 points, or 1.92%, to end at 7945.35. \par \par \uc2  Geoff Galbraith, vice-president of institutional sales at DBS Securities Hong Kong Ltd., said "the stronger Hong Kong dollar helped improve sentiment in the market." The Hong Kong dollar strengthened following Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chief Joseph Yam's clarification Monday that the Hong Kong dollar's exchange rate to US dollar will stay at HK$7.75, rather than fall to HK$7.80. \par \par \uc2  There were gains across the board, with all subindexes except the financial subindex posting gains. The financial subindex was dragged down by market heavyweight HSBC Holding PLC, traders said. \par \par \uc2  HSBC dipped HK$3.50, or 2.3%, to HK$147. "There are three reasons that HSBC shares were down," said Patrick Yiu, an analyst at Kingsway SW Securities Ltd. Yiu said investors are concerned about the bank's exposure to Latin America. "There was also selling pressure on HSBC in London," he said, adding that many merchant banks are hedging against the share price falling. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the share continues to be the focus of short-selling activities, which puts heavy pressure on the price. Short-selling turnover of HSBC reached HK$32 million Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, HSBC affiliate Hang Seng Bank Ltd.'s shares rose 50 cents, or 1.1%, to HK$46.90, despite news that the bank promised depositors holding Malaysian ringgit time deposits that it would make up any shortfall in the principal caused by the new Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du9f019rl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\pic