\par\par \b \uc2  China trade surplus surges, yuan strengthens. \b0\par\par\uc2 457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 1998\par 19:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING (Reuters) - China's trade surplus surged by 22.8 percent in the first eight months of this year, customs data showed on Friday, boosting the Chinese yuan in Shanghai trading and forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The surplus hit $31.3 billion in the January to August period, state television cited customs figures as showing. This compared with a surplus of $40.3 billion in 1997. \par \par \uc2  Exports rose a year-on-year 5.5 percent to $118.65 billion, far below export growth of 20.9 percent for all of 1997, but still robust considering the formidable challenge to exporters posed by the Asian financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  However, imports grew by just 0.4 percent to $87.26 billion. Imports for all of 1997 rose 2.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  The latest figures could put China on track to post another record trade surplus this year, economists said, even though export growth tailed off in August. \par \par \uc2  There were no figures for August alone, but economists estimated exports during the month at around $15.55 billion, lower than $16.027 billion a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  "People had expected the trade surplus to drop this year, but the trend now shows the surplus could rise," said Zhu Baoliang, an economist at the State Information Centre under State Development Planning Commission. \par \par \uc2  Zhu predicted exports would rise by about 5 percent for the whole year. \par \par \uc2  In Shanghai, dealers said the People's Bank of China sold yuan and bought dollars to stop the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising too rapidly. Last month the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  supported the yuan by selling dollars. \par \par \uc2  "This is much better than we expected," said one foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a Chinese state bank in Shanghai. \par \par \uc2  "There will be a fairly strong trade surplus for the full year." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was selling yuan at the 8.2790 level, dealers said. The yuan closed at 8.2790 to one dollar from 8.2796 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to keep the yuan stable," a second dealer said. "It does not want a sharp rise or a sharp fall." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment on Friday's reported \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  registered a trade surplus of $26.71 billion in the first seven months of this year, earlier customs figures show. The surplus in July alone was $4.2 billion. \par \par \uc2  China's exports to other Asian nations have been devastated by the Asian financial crisis, but exporters have been saved by strong demand in the United States and Europe. \par \par \uc2  Beijing has repeatedly said it has no need to devalue the yuan, despite the damage to its exports caused by a falling yen and the collapse of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  elsewhere in Asia. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9b00w0e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
