\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan ends up,  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  buys dollars. \b0\par\par\uc2 450 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 1998\par 06:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Sept 11 (Reuters) - The Chinese yuan closed higher against the dollar on Friday and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was believed to have sold the yuan at the 8.2790 level to slow the local unit's rise, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said there were widespread expectations that China's January-August trade data, due shortly, would show improvements over recently sluggish levels. \par \par \uc2  Domestic banks also were buying the yuan due to tighter curbs on purchases of dollars by local firms. Authorities have issued new restrictions on purchases of dollars to prepay foreign debt as a hedge against a possible yuan devaluation. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at 8.2790 to one dollar from 8.2796 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "There were signs that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was selling the yuan late in the session when the local unit rose above the 8.2790 level," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in August \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market several times last month but its actions then were to keep the yuan from weakening below the 8.2800 level. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to keep the yuan stable," another dealer said. "It does not want a sharp rise or a sharp fall." \par \par \uc2  Bank traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wanted to unwind some of its yuan positions built up when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's state television reported after the market closed that China recorded a $31.3 billion trade surplus in the first eight months of this year, up around 23 percent from the surplus of $25.4 billion in the same period last year. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the figures were better than the market had expected as some previous estimates had put China's foreign trade surplus for the full year at around $20 billion due to Asia's financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  Despite the strong trade data, traders said they believed the yuan would consolidate early next week. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yuan would still be pressured by factors such as unstable regional financial markets. \par \par \uc2  The local unit would move narrowly on the strong side of the key 8.2800 level early next week, they said. \par \par \uc2  The yuan opened at an intraday low of 8.2797 and hit a high of 8.2780. Most business was at 8.2796. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed down against the Japanese yen at an intraday low of 6.2037 to 100 yen from 6.1238. It ended up against the Hong Kong dollar at a high of 1.0670 to one dollar from 1.0677. \par \par \uc2  - Shanghai Newsroom: (86) 21-6355-4009; Fax 21-6355-5015 \par \par \uc2  - Email: reutersh@uninet.com.cn \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9b00vn1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
