\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  Editorial Says HK Justified To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  In Mkts \b0\par\par\uc2 187 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 1998\par 04:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BEIJING (Dow Jones)--The current volatility in Hong Kong's stock and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets is indirectly the result of U.S. and Japanese reluctance to prop up the yen, a Chinese newspaper editorial Friday said. \par \par \uc2  The China Daily carried comments from an analyst at a state-run think tank, Wei Yanshen, who defended the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the government's decision to defend the linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  "The severe economic climate has caused Hong Kong's economy to suffer a lot. Due to the reluctance of the U.S. and Japan to end devaluation of the yen, economic recovery in the region has been postponed. Overspeculation has led to the present financial situation in Hong Kong," the editorial said. \par \par \uc2  It said Hong Kong's government is "absolutely justified" to buy shares and futures. "These measures are not directed indifferently against all speculative activities," said Wei, who is a senior fellow of the Institute of Asia-Pacific studies under the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 28-08-98 \par \par \uc2  0230GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8s07d0p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
