\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  no departure from HK principles-Yam. \b0\par\par\uc2 431 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Aug 24 (Reuters) - Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) chief Joseph Yam said on Monday there was no contradiction in the government becoming a market player if such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was for the good of the economy. \par \par \uc2  In an article in the South \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Morning Post, Yam defended the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock and futures markets this month by citing examples when the government had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past. \par \par \uc2  "In the monetary and financial systems, there is significant \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the form of the regulation of financial markets and the supervision of financial institution," Yam said. \par \par \uc2  "Indeed there are many examples of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. \par \par \uc2  The article was the latest to be published in foreign and local newspapers explaining why the government had asked the HKMA to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in stock and futures markets to deter speculators. \par \par \uc2  Yam invoked statements by Hong Kong's former financial secretary, Sir Philip Haddon-Cave. \par \par \uc2  Haddon-Cave argued in the late 1970s that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was sometimes necessary because "imperfections in the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the market mechanism (may lead) to economic inefficiency". \par \par \uc2  Yam said: "Consistent with Sir Philip's view that \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionism\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be 'relevant to the maintenance of the economic stability in Hong Kong', and with Exchange Fund Advisory Committee backing, Financial Secretary Donald Tsang made the order to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the free play of market forces." \par \par \uc2  Yam thumbed his nose at critics of the government's action and indicated that speculators were up against an administration that was "run by people who have put Hong Kong on the map as the freest economy in the world". \par \par \uc2  "It is so easy to wave the banner of free markets and condemn any government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and be seen to be righteous and knowledgeable; notwithstanding that the government concerned has been so successfully running the freest economy in the world," Yam said. \par \par \uc2  "But we must be humble and thank those wanting to lecture us on free markets, if only for the purpose of keeping us on our toes," he said in the article punctuated with sarcasm. \par \par \uc2  Yam repeated Hong Kong's resolve at keeping its exchange rate linked with the U.S. dollar. "This resolve has never been stronger," he said. \par \par \uc2  "As the financial storm in this region spreads to almost everywhere now, and with increased intensity, the discipline of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board arrangements of our linked exchange rate system is our best safeguard for our long term prosperity and stability." \par \par \uc2  - Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843 6441; Fax (852) 2845 0636 \par \par \uc2  - Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8o01dkh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
