\par\par \b \uc2  China won't devalue in next 1-2 yrs - official. \b0\par\par\uc2 266 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1998\par 07:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, Aug 20 (Reuters) - China will not devalue its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the next one to two years, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "In my opinion, the yuan will not devalue within the next one to two years," Li Fuxiang, deputy director of the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told a seminar on the effects of the Asian financial crisis on the Chinese economy. \par \par \uc2  A devaluation would "destroy confidence in monetary policy, causing stronger expectation of further devaluations" and would not necessarily boost exports, Li said. \par \par \uc2  A devaluation could also wreak havoc with stock and property prices, hit domestic consumption, trigger inflation and hurt investor confidence in China, he said. \par \par \uc2  Beijing has repeatedly vowed not to devalue the yuan but its resolve in maintaining a stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been tested as Asia's economic woes drag on. \par \par \uc2  It has also warned Japan to take measures to support the sliding yen, saying that further falls in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would put more pressure on the yuan. \par \par \uc2  The prospect of a yuan devaluation has spooked international financial markets, which fear such a move would spark further slides in other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and drag the region deeper into economic recession. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the tightly controlled \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market several times over the past few months to support the yuan amid worries over the fate of the yen. \par \par \uc2  ($1.0 = 8.3 yuan) \par \par \uc2  - Beijing Newsroom (86) 10-6532-1921; Fax (86) 10-6532-4978 \par \par \uc2  - Email: beijing.newsroomzreuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8k017kp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
