\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2 's/Newspapers/Yen -2: Calls For U.S.  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 276 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1998\par 06:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The People's Daily, the flagship newspaper of the Chinese Communist party, also called on the U.S. to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prop up the yen, and blamed the U.S. and Japan for allowing the yen to weaken. \par \par \uc2  The article accused the U.S. and Japan of being only concerned about short-term economic benefits a weak yen provides to their economies and not taking the welfare of Asia's crisis-stricken countries into consideration. \par \par \uc2  "As the number one and two global economic powers, the U.S. and Japan shouldn't only ask other countries to adopt a responsible attitude to prevent the worsening of the Asian financial crisis, while these two countries adopt irresponsible attitudes," the editorial said. \par \par \uc2  Chinese media recently have called Japan irresponsible for letting its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaken, while touting China's own efforts to keep the yuan steady. \par \par \uc2  However, the Asian financial crisis, the sliding yen, and China's own slowing economy have raised fears that China will devalue the yuan to boost economic growth. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, officials have pledged to keep the yuan exchange rate stable despite the yen's negative effects on China's export growth and foreign investment. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said recently a devaluation won't boost exports because over half of China's exports rely on imported materials that would become more expensive after a yuan depreciation. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan can only be converted under the current account for trade-related purposes, so a decision to devalue could only be made by the government. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 20-08-98 \par \par \uc2  0422GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8k072kf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
