\par\par \b \uc2  China yuan unshaken by floods, but eye on Japan. \b0\par\par\uc2  By William Kazer \par \uc2 705 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 1998\par 11:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Aug 18 (Reuters) - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unshaken by floods and a shock wave from Russia's rouble but \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said on Tuesday the key to Beijing's exchange rate policy was the Japanese yen. \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, barely blinked on the Shanghai-based China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trading System, shrugging off news of an effective devaluation of the rouble and mounting economic losses from domestic floods. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the twin blows may have raised anxiety levels in dealing rooms around the world but the real alarm bells were likely to be sounded from Japan. \par \par \uc2  "Keep your eye on the yen," said Li Yiyuan, an economist at Qinghua University in Beijing. \par \par \uc2  "Japan is a major trading partner and that market is important to China." \par \par \uc2  China's Premier Zhu Rongji has pledged not to devalue, despite the loss of export competitiveness to other Asian countries benefiting from much weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  So far, Zhu has proved to have a bit more credibility than Russian President Boris Yeltsin or many Asian counterparts who have made the same claims of support for their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  only to backpedal later. \par \par \uc2  "Pressure is not as great in China as it appears to be from the outside," said a dealer with a Shanghai bank. \par \par \uc2  "We really don't see a devaluation of the yuan this year." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has showed no signs of a lessening its resolve to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable despite the latest turn of events. \par \par \uc2  "Neither the rouble nor the floods will have an impact on our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy," a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the yuan traded narrowly and finished at 8.2799 to the dollar, little changed from the 8.2798 level on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tightly managed \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading system on Tuesday despite several recent moves by the bank to shore up the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  They said the roubles woes no doubt would raise psychological pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that have not buckled under global financial strains but Russia remained a minor factor in China's trade and financial picture. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, domestic share prices steadied on Tuesday after slumping badly on Monday due to fears over the massive flooding in central and northeastern China. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said they feared that the flooding would cut into economic growth, already showing signs of slowing below a targeted 8.0 percent for this year. \par \par \uc2  China is seeing its exports trail off while domestic demand is sluggish - just as it wants growth to create new jobs for millions of workers likely to be laid off in an overhaul of state industry. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said China still had too much to lose from a devaluation. \par \par \uc2  "At the moment, it is not in China's interest to devalue," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a Shanghai financial institution. \par \par \uc2  The greatest danger was it would risk undermining confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which could trigger a run on state banks, as worried citizens turn to the black market to protect their savings. \par \par \uc2  It would also deal a blow to the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar, a political embarrassment Beijing was eager to avoid. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, it would likely trigger another round of competitive devaluations around Asia and have little benefit to China's exports, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "A devaluation is not a magic pill," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer with a domestic financial institution. \par \par \uc2  For the time beiing, Beijing could wield its mighty club of $140 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to defend its interests. \par \par \uc2  But Beijing is looking to Japan to play a role in an Asian recovery and it wants a stronger yen to pull in imports from China and the rest of the region. \par \par \uc2  Beijing has been irked that Japan does not seem to be willing to shoulder what it views as a fair share of the regional economic burden. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said that China could take another look at the gains and losses from a change in policy next year. \par \par \uc2  "If the yen weakens substantially, authorities could review their options sometime next year," said a trader. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8i011pe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
