\par\par \b \uc2  Chinese Yuan Ends -3: Yuan Pledge Seen "Iron-Solid" \b0\par\par\uc2 278 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 1998\par 06:43\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  "The action (of the HKMA) shows that China's policy of keeping its \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable is iron-solid," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader on the exchange. \par \par \uc2  Though China resumed sovereignty over Hong Kong last year, Beijing has vowed not to meddle in the former British territory's affairs, including financial matters. \par \par \uc2  Still, traders here said Monday they believed Beijing was in close contact with the HKMA concerning its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some even said they thought \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had encouraged the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a way to curtail speculative activity in Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is pegged to the dollar. Speculative action in the Hong Kong dollar has fueled concern over the yuan, which Beijing has pledged repeatedly not to devalue. Foreign analysts say that it's unlikely \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  was actively encouraging the HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though most say Beijing was likely given forewarning. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they expect the yuan to remain stable for the rest of 1998. They said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's pledge not to devalue has been widely accepted here, but note also that concern remains high about the course of the yuan in 1999. That's particularly true amid signs that the domestic economy is faltering. \par \par \uc2  China's growth slowed to 7% in the first half of 1998, below the government's own target of 8%. Some analysts say growth in the third quarter will be even slower as the devastating impact of flooding in the middle of the country hits. Many economists say that domestic growth remains the lynch pin of Beijing's pledge for a stable yuan. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  17-08-98 \par \par \uc2  0443GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020010914du8h00r9c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
