\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2 's People's Daily Continues Call For Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 333 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 1998\par 08:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BEIJING (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top newspaper Friday continued recent calls for the Japanese and U.S. governments to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prop up the yen. \par \par \uc2  An editorial in the People's Daily, the flagship newspaper of the Chinese Communist Party, echoed media statements of the past few days accusing the U.S. and Japan of using the the weak yen to boost their own economies. \par \par \uc2  Japan wants a low yen exchange rate to fuel exports while the U.S. wants a strong dollar to maintain the bullish trend in its stock markets, the article said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S., as the world's biggest economic power, and Japan, as the world's largest holder of foreign reserves, can afford to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, it said. \par \par \uc2  Japan can also raise its rock-bottom interest rates to drive up the yen, the editorial said. \par \par \uc2  "As long as Japan and the U.S. take the whole Asian economy into consideration and earnestly work to stabilize the yen, the yen's slide can be controlled," it said. "Therefore, everyone is watching Japan and the U.S." \par \par \uc2  Since the yen fell to record lows against the dollar earlier this week, Chinese state media, whose content are approved by the government, have renewed criticisms against Japan and the U.S. for not doing enough to halt the yen's slump. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, officials have pledged to keep the yuan exchange rate stable despite the yen's negative effects on China's export growth and foreign investment. A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said Tuesday a devaluation won't boost exports because over half of China's exports rely on imported materials that would become more expensive after a yuan depreciation. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan can only be converted under the current account for trade-related purposes, so a decision to devalue could only be made by the Beijing government. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 14-08-98 \par \par \uc2  0621GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8e06x4a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
