\par\par \b \uc2  China's State-Run Media Blame Weak Yen For Undermining Yuan \b0\par\par\uc2 778 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1998\par 17:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BEIJING -(Dow Jones)- Chinese state media Thursday continued to lob strong criticism at the Japanese government, blaming weakness in the yen for eroding domestic confidence in the yuan. \par \par \uc2  "Continuing depreciations of the yen has clearly added pressure to the yuan," said a signed editorial in the China Business Times. \par \par \uc2  Although the government has repeatedly said it won't devalue the yuan, Chinese foreign-trade companies have been increasingly hoarding more of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings in fear of a devaluation, the article said. \par \par \uc2  In addition, declining confidence in the yuan has shown up in the country's growing black-market trade, where the dollar has fetched around 9.00 yuan, compared with the official rate of 8.28 yuan, the report said. \par \par \uc2  The yen's continued weakness has also rattled equity markets around the world, including China's own stock exchanges, it added. China's B-shares, or shares technically reserved for foreigners, have sold off sharply in recent weeks as the unstable yen and China's own slowing economy have kept investors away. \par \par \uc2  "Whether the yuan will be devalued depends on whether the yen will weaken further," said another editorial in the International Business Daily, which is published by the Ministry of Foreign Trade & Economic Cooperation. \par \par \uc2  Recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep the yuan stable "indicate that the weak yen may possibly force the yuan to devalue," the article said. "But if the yuan falls, the consequences would be unbearable." \par \par \uc2  That's because a yuan depreciation could push regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  into another free fall, exacerbate the Asia crisis, undermine the Hong Kong dollar peg and even spread market instability to the rest of the world. \par \par \uc2  The yen needs to stay between 140 yen and 150 yen to the dollar, the newspaper said. Anything below 150 yen would be "unacceptable to everyone" and bring in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears, it added. \par \par \uc2  However, it predicted that the yen will remain weak in the next three to six months, with the dollar trading in range of 130 yen to 160 yen. \par \par \uc2  Since the yen fell to eight-year lows against the dollar this week, Chinese state media, whose content is approved by government officials, have renewed criticisms against Japan and the U.S. in increasingly stronger language. \par \par \uc2  Several newspapers Wednesday accused the U.S. and Japan of complicity in the yen's recent slide and urged both countries to once again directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prop up the yen as they did in June. Chinese media have also called Japan irresponsible for letting its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaken and hurting regional economies, while touting China's own efforts to keep the yuan steady. \par \par \uc2  "Japan, as a global economic power, has tried to improve its own export competitiveness without caring about the survival of other countries," the China Business Times editorial said. "This is a despicable act." \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, top officials from President Jiang Zemin down have said China has acted responsibly and has "paid a price" for its stable yuan policy. \par \par \uc2  Officials say Japan's lagging economy has hurt China's exports and foreign investment and made achieving a targeted 8% gross domestic product growth more difficult this year. Japan is one of China's biggest export markets and a main source of foreign investment. \par \par \uc2  But Gakuwa Yamagata at Nikko Research in Hong Kong dismissed recent criticisms of Japan in state media as political posturing ahead of a Sino-Japanese presidential summit scheduled for next month. \par \par \uc2  The media reports are "a political threat against Japan," Yamagata said. "They want to show that they are closely monitoring the Japanese economy." \par \par \uc2  China's strong trade surplus, at $26.71 billion at the end of July, will help keep the yuan stable, he added. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, a top Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official - while acknowledging that domestic confidence in the yuan has eroded - said that a yuan depreciation wouldn't help boost China's slowing economy and would cause other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fall. \par \par \uc2  "Countries can't rely on competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations to boost their economies," the China Business Times editorial said. "It would only make the situation worse." \par \par \uc2  China's yuan can only be converted under the current account for trade-related purposes, so a decision to devalue would be made by the government. The dollar was at 8.2798 yuan late Thursday in Beijing compared with 8.2800 yuan Wednesday. Around the same time, the dollar was at 145.84 yen. \par \par \uc2  -I-Chun Chen; 8610 6532-6652; ichen@ap.org \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du8d015k4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
