\par\par \b \uc2  China central banker says no need to devalue yuan. \b0\par\par\uc2 378 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 1998\par 05:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, Aug 11 (Reuters) - China does not need to devalue its yuan, or renminbi, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Liu Mingkang, deputy governor of the People's Bank of China, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "The renminbi does not need to be devalued and will not be devalued," Liu told reporters. \par \par \uc2  While a devaluation could help boost exports, China would pay the price in higher costs for imported raw materials and components and inflation, Liu said. \par \par \uc2  "A drastic devaluation could bring about a short-term export recovery but it will not help China's long-term strategic competitiveness," Liu said. \par \par \uc2  "A devaluation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will also lead to a loss of confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the public and will also bring about inflation," Liu said. \par \par \uc2  "We think that devaluation or minor adjustment of the exchange rate is not a good measure for China," Liu said. \par \par \uc2  Economists say a yuan devaluation could spark another round of devastating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations across Asia and further drag the region's beleaguered economies into recession. \par \par \uc2  "A drastic devaluation will surely deal a severe blow to other countries and economies, particularly those already suffering from financial crisis," Liu said. \par \par \uc2  Although Beijing has repeatedly pledged not to devalue the yuan, Chinese and regional markets have been spooked by fears that it could do so if the Japanese yen continued to weaken. \par \par \uc2  "We are fully capable of maintaining the exchange rate of the renminbi," Liu said. \par \par \uc2  "We will adopt necessary measures to maintain the stability of the yuan exchange rate," Liu said. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the tightly controlled \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the yuan amid worries over the fate of the Japanese yen. \par \par \uc2  The bank has waded into the market several times over the past few months to support the yuan. \par \par \uc2  Beijing has also voiced displeasure with Tokyo's inability to enact policies to support the yen and help speed the recovery of Asian economies. \par \par \uc2  Asked how much the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had spent on defending the yuan, Liu said: "I can't disclose the figures". \par \par \uc2  Liu said foreign and domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators were "not wise in their practise". \par \par \uc2  "I advise speculators not to miscalculate," he said without elaborating. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8b02v5z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
