\par\par \b \uc2  China B stocks at new lows on yuan fears, floods. \b0\par\par\uc2 611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 1998\par 13:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Aug 10 (Reuters) - China's B shares for foreign investors closed at record lows on Monday on fears that weakness in the Japanese yen might cause Beijing to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts and brokers said. \par \par \uc2  Floods ravaging central China contributed to the negative mood as investors dumped shares on worries the disaster would hurt listed companies and the economy overall, they said. \par \par \uc2  Shenzhen's B share index slumped 2.11 points, or 3.66 percent, to a record closing low of 55.46 on turnover of HK$12 million ($1.53 million) compared with Friday's HK$13 million. \par \par \uc2  Shanghai's B index tumbled 0.984 point, or 3.44 percent, to a record low close of 27.634 as turnover shrank to $2.02 million from $5.39 million on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The yen is still a big factor today," said an analyst at United Securities in Shenzhen. "And there has not been a single B share company that has published good results so far." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the tightly controlled \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday to support the yuan amid fears that Beijing might devalue the local unit if the Japanese yen continued to weaken. \par \par \uc2  Beijing has repeatedly pledged to support the yuan but it also has been irked by Japan's inability to enact policies that could halt the slide in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  It sees Japan as failing to shoulder its fair share of the burden in pulling Asia's slack economies out of their current difficulties. \par \par \uc2  Poor interim results announced over the weekend by a number of B share firms also took their toll, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  In Shenzhen, Changan Automobile was the most actively traded issue and among the biggest losers after announcing on Saturday that first-half earnings plunged 57 percent from the same period last year. \par \par \uc2  The mini-car maker fell its 10 percent limit of HK$0.08 to end at HK$0.68 on volume of 1.35 million shares. \par \par \uc2  Shanghai-listed Jiangsu Wuling Diesel Engine Co on Saturday reported 1998 interim net profits of 30.485 million yuan. \par \par \uc2  The company did not give figures for the first half of 1997, but it recorded net profits of 108.63 million yuan for all of last year. Wuling closed down $0.004, or 3.13 percent, to $0.124 on volume of 237,000 shares on Monday. \par \par \uc2  China's domestic A shares also finished down on concerns about the stability of the yuan and flooding on the Yangtze River, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  News that the government had destroyed some secondary dikes along the swollen waterway to protect larger cities caused fears floods would disrupt the national economy, they said. \par \par \uc2  Shenzhen's A share index fell 12.88 points, or 3.15 percent, to 396.51 on turnover of 2.7 billion yuan ($325.30 million), shrinking from 3.2 billion yuan on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Shanghai's A index lost 38.779 points, or 2.87 percent, to 1,314.537 as turnover contracted to 3.3 billion yuan against 4.3 billion yuan on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Wuhan Xianglong Power Industry Co was the biggest A share loser on the Shanghai exchange after listing 12.59 million employee-held shares on Monday. Brokers said selling was linked to the listing of the new shares and worries that flooding might affect the power producer as floodwaters threatened the major industrial city of Wuhan. \par \par \uc2  The issue lost 1.61 yuan, or 9.91 percent, to 14.64 yuan on volume of 682,659 shares. \par \par \uc2  ($1=HK$7.8) ($1=8.3 yuan). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8a02uec\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
