\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr/yen bid in morning Europe, eyes June highs. \b0\par\par\uc2 721 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 1998\par 10:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 10 (Reuters) - The dollar was firm in Europe on Monday, rising close to this year's high against the yen as the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  took another beating amid concern about weak Tokyo stocks and speculation of a Chinese yuan devaluation. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was nervous ahead of 146.75 yen, the June 16 high, with profit-taking pushing the dollar lower each time it looked set to approach that peak. \par \par \uc2  The level, the dollar's highest in nearly eight years, is meeting tough resistance but many dealers expect the ceiling to be eroded with a move toward the 150 psychological level with no end in sigh to bearish news out of Japan. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's rise was spurred in Asian trade by news that unlisted Japanese copier maker Mita Industrial Co Ltd announced it had filed for court protection from its creditors, with liabilities exceeding 200 billion yen ($1.36 billion). \par \par \uc2  The news increased worries about further insolvencies in Japan, hurting sentiment on Japanese stocks, with the Nikkei closing down 1.3 percent. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said concern about Japan's slowness in fixing its banking system was spooking the markets. One hurdle facing the reform efforts of Japan's new government is the possibility of a rocky passage in the Diet for its banking sector reform bills. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese problems remain at the forefront with the opposition taking a hard line on the total plan for the banking system and the copier maker seeking court protection," said Rob Hayward, economic adviser at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  At 0750 GMT the dollar was at 146.50/60 yen and 1.7810/20 marks, compared with 146.25/35 and 1.7805/15 in late European trading on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Caution that the Bank of Japan might step into the market in the yen's defense kept the dollar's gains somewhat slow above 146.50 yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Speculation about a Chinese yuan devaluation and a possible de-linking of the Hong Kong and the U.S. dollars was likely to keep the dollar solid against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Chinese President Jiang Zemin reiterated on Sunday that Beijing was determined to avoid devaluaing the yuan despite a slowing economy. \par \par \uc2  "Despite no action to devalue the yuan over the weekend, speculation continues to be rife that the Chinese authorities will have to adjust their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in light of the deterioration in their competitive position over the past 12 months," said Michael Lewis, international economist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell in London. \par \par \uc2  The People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week, and supported the yuan again on Monday, dealers said. The slide of the yen and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has put Chinese exports at a competitive disadvantage. \par \par \uc2  The latest yen weakness has been sparked by scepticism that the government of Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi can quickly implement measures to kick-start the economy. \par \par \uc2  Obuchi outlined plans for stimulating the world's second-biggest economy on Friday and said any recovery could take two years. The economy is in a prolonged slump and in a severe situation, Obuchi said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese officials embarked on another round of discussion about the yen's weakness. Dealers said the comments added to some caution ahead of the June high. The Bank of Japan and the Federal Reserve had jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the dollar hit its June high at 146.75 yen. \par \par \uc2  Senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Haruhiko Kuroda said recent moves in the yen are unfavourable for Japan as well as for Asia and the world economy. \par \par \uc2  The extent of capital outflows out of Japan was highlighted by figures released on Monday showing net foreign bond buying by Japanese investors rose sharply in July compared with June to 1.23 trillion yen after net purchases of 507.5 billion. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stuck to tight trading range around 1.78 marks. Pan-German inflation rose 0.9 percent year-on-year in July, the lowest since records began in 1991, and was up 0.3 percent on the month. \par \par \uc2  The mark was little changed against the yen at 82.25/28 from 82.13/15. \par \par \uc2  ((Astrid Zweynert London newsroom + 44 171 542 7792 fax +44 171 542 5293, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8a02szf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
