\par\par \b \uc2  S.Korea's won closes lower on yen, yuan woes. \b0\par\par\uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 1998\par 10:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Aug 10 (Reuters) - South Korea's won closed lower against the dollar on Monday over concerns the yen's weakness and a possible devaluation of the Chinese yuan will induce the government to encourage a depreciation of the won. \par \par \uc2  The won closed at 1,338 per dollar from Friday's close of 1,326. It opened at 1,355 and moved between 1,336 and 1,362. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said exporters, who had been waiting for the dollar to rise, sold dollars in the belief that its gains would be capped. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar holdings with the country's exporters should not be underestimated," said a Citibank dealer. \par \par \uc2  The yen was trading at 146.57/62 at 0744 GMT, compared with Friday's close of 145.53 in Tokyo market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a gloomy forecast for the Japanese economy would put downward pressure on the yen and that would force China to devalue its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Yangtze river would add pressure to the yuan's depreciation, they said. \par \par \uc2  The yen-yuan factor hit most other Asian markets on Monday, as the region shuddered over a return of the kind of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil that shook Asia last year. \par \par \uc2  The dollar surged to near eight-year highs against the yen in morning Tokyo trade. Other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , notably the Malaysian ringgit, also sagged, dragging stocks with them. \par \par \uc2  "The weaker yen fuelled concern that it may reignite devaluations of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially the Chinese yuan and the Hong Kong dollar," said Akihiro Naemura, a strategist at Okasan Securities Co Ltd. \par \par \uc2  Markets appeared to have shrugged off a weekend assurance by Chinese President Jiang Zemin that Beijing was determined to keep a stable yuan despite Asia's financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  Comments on Monday morning by a Japanese finance ministry official expressing concern about the yen's weakness had more of an impact, helping the yen back towards 146 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But by mid-afternoon it was back on the slide at around 146.25 to the dollar and traders were pessimistic about its prospects. \par \par \uc2  "It's a matter of time before the dollar breaks through 146.75 yen," said one European dealer. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was growing concern that floods which have devastated parts of China would have a major impact on the economy and bring forward a competitive devaluation of the yuan. \par \par \uc2  "There is concern about the flooding in China, because if great damage is done to the dikes in big cities or important areas, the Chinese economy will definitely be affected," said Ben Wang, head of research at \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Everbright Securities. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid fears that Beijing may devalue the yuan, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The People's Bank of China bought the local unit in the tightly controlled market to hold the yuan at 8.2800 to the U.S. dollar, the third time since July 31. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a depreciation of the yen and yuan would hurt South Korean exporters, who have to compete with their Japanese and Chinese rivals in overseas markets. \par \par \uc2  But exporters will start cashing in their dollar earnings when the dollar starts to rise and that should keep the won from falling steeply, they said. \par \par \uc2  The Citibank dealer forecast the won-dollar rate would range from 1,320 to 1,350 on Tuesday, while a dealer at Korea Exchange Bank put it between 1,320 and 1,360. \par \par \uc2  ((Kim Myong-hwan, Seoul Newsroom +82-2 727 5645, fax +82-2 727 5666, seoul.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8a02sxa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
